---
import Base from '../../../../layouts/Base.astro';

const SITE = 'https://precisionsolutionstech.com';
const title = 'TrailSift for iPhone & iPad Privacy Policy | Precision Solutions Tech';
const description =
  'How TrailSift for iPhone and iPad handles your trail camera photos. Detection runs on your device; your photos are never uploaded. Optional wind and weather use Apple Weather.';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${SITE}/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Privacy Policy', item: `${SITE}/legal/privacy/` },
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 3,
        name: 'TrailSift for iPhone & iPad',
        item: `${SITE}/legal/privacy/trailsift-ios/`,
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    name: 'TrailSift for iPhone & iPad Privacy Policy',
    url: `${SITE}/legal/privacy/trailsift-ios/`,
    description,
    dateModified: '2026-10-14',
    isPartOf: { '@type': 'WebSite', name: 'Precision Solutions Tech', url: `${SITE}/` },
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/legal/privacy/trailsift-ios/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <ol>
        <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
        <li><a href="/legal/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a></li>
        <li>TrailSift for iPhone &amp; iPad</li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <article class="prose">
      <h1>TrailSift for iPhone &amp; iPad Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="meta">Last updated: October 14, 2026</p>
      <p class="lead">
        This Privacy Policy describes how <strong>TrailSift</strong> (“we”, “us”) handles information
        when you use the TrailSift app for iPhone and iPad (the “App”), published by
        <strong>Precision Solutions Tech</strong>, operated by <strong>SG TECHNOLOGY LLC</strong>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="summary">Short version</h2>
      <p>
        TrailSift works <strong>on your device, with no signal needed</strong>. It reads the memory
        cards you open, looks for animals with on-device models, and keeps the photos you choose in the
        App. Nothing is uploaded to us. We operate no account system and no servers that receive your
        data. The only optional network feature is wind and weather for your sightings, from Apple
        Weather (see below).
      </p>

      <h2 id="local">What the App processes on your device</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Photos and videos on memory cards or folders you open, and the information inside them
          (capture time, camera model, the date and temperature stamp printed on the photo)
        </li>
        <li>Results of on-device detection, species identification, and photo sharpness</li>
        <li>The cameras, tags, buck names, and notes you add</li>
        <li>Your settings, and the location (country and US state) you set for each camera</li>
        <li>GPS coordinates some cameras write into their photos, used only to find the camera’s location</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        All of this stays on your device. Photos you import are stored in the App’s storage and are
        included in your device backups (iCloud Backup or computer backup) like other app data.
      </p>

      <h2 id="location">Location</h2>
      <p>
        Each camera has a location (a country, and a state in the US) that tells TrailSift which
        animals can live there, and optionally a spot (rounded to about 1 km) used for wind and weather.
        If you tap “Use my current location”, the App asks iOS for your position once, turns it into a
        country and state using a map stored inside the App, and saves the country, state and rounded
        spot with the camera. Your exact position is not stored. A camera’s spot can also come from GPS
        written into its photos or a pin you place on a map, and can be cleared at any time. The App
        does not track your location in the background. You can pick a location from a list instead,
        or turn off location access in iOS Settings.
      </p>

      <h2 id="weather">Wind and weather (optional)</h2>
      <p>
        If “Wind &amp; weather for sightings” is on (Settings), then whenever your device has a
        connection the App asks Apple Weather (WeatherKit) for the historical weather at each camera’s
        spot for the times of your sightings. Apple receives the camera’s spot rounded to about 1 km
        and the date range. Your photos are not sent. To look up a camera’s time zone, the App also asks
        Apple’s geocoder about that rounded spot. Turn the setting off to stop all of this; scanning and
        everything else keep working.
      </p>

      <h2 id="photos-app">Photos app</h2>
      <p>
        If you choose “Save to Photos”, the App asks for permission to add items to your Photos library
        and, if you allow full access, creates albums named after your cameras. The App does not read
        the rest of your Photos library.
      </p>

      <h2 id="purchases">Purchases</h2>
      <p>
        The one-time unlock is sold through Apple’s App Store. Apple handles payment; we do not receive
        your payment details or Apple Account information. The App checks your purchase with Apple on
        your device.
      </p>

      <h2 id="analytics">Analytics, tracking, and advertising</h2>
      <p>
        The App contains <strong>no analytics SDKs, no advertising SDKs, and no third-party
        trackers</strong>. We do not profile you, and we do not sell or share personal information. If
        that ever changes, this policy will be updated before the change ships.
      </p>

      <h2 id="network">Network access</h2>
      <p>
        Scanning, importing, tagging, insights, and clearing cards do not need internet access. The App
        uses the network only through Apple’s systems: App Store purchases, and, if you leave it on,
        Apple Weather and Apple’s geocoder for wind and weather.
      </p>

      <h2 id="people">Photos of people</h2>
      <p>
        Trail cameras sometimes photograph people. TrailSift labels them so you can find them; it does
        not identify who they are. You are responsible for how you keep and share such photos.
      </p>

      <h2 id="retention">Data retention</h2>
      <p>
        Your library stays on your device until you delete photos, use “Erase all TrailSift data” in
        Settings, or delete the App. If you contact support, we keep your message as long as needed to
        help you.
      </p>

      <h2 id="children">Children’s privacy</h2>
      <p>The App is not directed to children under 13 (or the minimum age in your jurisdiction).</p>

      <h2 id="changes">Changes</h2>
      <p>We may update this policy. The “Last updated” date will change when we do.</p>

      <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Privacy questions:
        <a href="mailto:support@precisionsolutionstech.com">support@precisionsolutionstech.com</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/ios-apps/trailsift/">TrailSift for iPhone &amp; iPad</a>
        ·
        <a href="/ios-apps/trailsift/support/">Support</a>
        ·
        <a href="/legal/privacy/">Company privacy policy</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
